3217"} "Erika Salumäe"] " of Estonia, who had only made the quarters by advancing via the repêchage. Ballanger would defeat her in two straight races, and would not lose a race en route to the gold medal. In the final she defeated Ferris, who had advanced by defeating the 1992 bronze medalist, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81252"} "Ingrid Haringa"] " (NED), in the semis. Haringa repeated as bronze medalist, defeating the 1992 silver medalist, Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/13184"} "Annett Neumann"] ". Over the next few years, Ballanger would establish herself as the top female sprinter in the world, winning the World Championships in 1996-1999 and defending her gold medal at Sydney in 2000."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "R1 Rep"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/13061"} "Félicia Ballanger"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "11.277 (2)"] [:td {} "11.901 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/13107"} "Michelle Ferris"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]] [:td {} "11.212 (1)"] [:td {} "11.923 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "0 (2 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81252"} "Ingrid Haringa"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED